"executive_summary": "This study identifies the sarco/endoplasmic reticulum calcium-ATPase (SERCA) as a critical host factor facilitating replication of paramyxoviruses, specifically Peste des petits ruminants virus (PPRV) and Newcastle disease virus (NDV). The authors utilized Thapsigargin, a specific SERCA inhibitor, to demonstrate that blocking SERCA activity significantly reduces viral replication without causing cytotoxicity at the tested concentration. Mechanistic assays revealed that SERCA inhibition specifically impairs viral entry and the proper subcellular localization of viral proteins, preventing their transport from the cytoplasm to the cell surface. Furthermore, the study observed that viral infection itself induces SERCA expression, which can be blocked by the inhibitor. A notable finding is that while PPRV remained sensitive to the inhibitor, long-term passage of NDV in the presence of Thapsigargin led to the emergence of a partially resistant mutant, suggesting viruses can evolve to bypass host factor dependencies. These findings position SERCA as a promising target for broad-spectrum antiviral therapeutics against paramyxoviruses.",
